From 5a575f17ee92a25ad41b49805562482fabc5603d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ahmad Mahmoud <alshahia@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 16:17:24 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Q4 (perf/queue-controls-gpu-codec): TF32 + async prefetch
 + GPU util telemetry

Closes the third and final functional gap surfaced in the original
hardware review. After this commit the GUI is using every hardware
acceleration we have available on RTX 4000 (CUDA 12.6, PyTorch 2.12
+cu126): TF32 for fp32 ops, TensorRT for fp16 inference, NVDEC for
decode, NVENC for encode, async background-thread prefetch, and a
1 Hz GPU util telemetry feed the GUI can render.

What changed
------------

TF32 (`pipeline/tensors.py`):
  * Module-level next to the existing `cudnn.benchmark = True`:
        torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 = True
        torch.backends.cudnn.allow_tf32 = True
    Both wrapped in try/except (older PyTorch builds may not expose the
    attribute). TF32 is a 19-bit float that runs at fp32 throughput and
    lands within ~3 decimal places of true fp32. cuDNN picks the path
    automatically per-op:
      * The recommended model runs fp16, so the heavy convs are
        unaffected -- TF32 only kicks in for the residual fp32 ops
        (small matmuls, channel shuffle). Measured PSNR delta < 0.01 dB
        per the RTX 4000 speed-up report.
      * Turing (SM 75) and earlier accept the flag but no-ops it
        because the kernels aren't implemented in hardware -- safe on
        every NVIDIA card we care about.

Async prefetch default (`settings.py`):
  * `Defaults.prefetch` flips from "sync" to "async". The async reader
    has been stable for a while; the sync default was the legacy
    conservative choice. Async adds a background decoder thread that
    fills a bounded queue while the GPU runs the model -- the obvious
    win on any modern multi-core host.
  * Existing settings.json files that pin `prefetch=sync` still load
    that value -- the default change only affects fresh installs / reset
    settings.

GPU util telemetry (`pipeline/worker.py`):
  * New `_read_gpu_util()` helper that probes pynvml once (or
    `torch.cuda.mem_get_info()` as a fallback). Process-cached so the
    cost is paid only on the first emission.
  * New `_maybe_emit_gpu_util(emit_fn, job_id, last_emit_ref)` helper
    that rate-limits emissions to >= 1 Hz regardless of caller cadence.
    The closure takes a single-element list (`[last_emit_time]`) so the
    worker can hand the helper a slot in an existing ref-list -- no
    need to thread the timestamp through every helper signature.
  * Both `_run_video` progress sites (per-frame loop + batched path)
    now piggy-back a gpu_util emission on the existing 2 Hz progress
    tick. The worker doesn't spin up its own polling thread; the helper
    is essentially free (one int read + one dict lookup).
  * New `JobEvent(kind="gpu_util", job_id=..., message="util=X%
    mem=Y/ZMB t=TC")` payload -- reuses the existing `JobEvent.message`
    field so we don't grow the dataclass for one optional signal.

GUI consumption (`app.py`, `widgets/gpu_monitor.py`):
  * `_handle_event` learns a new `evt.kind == "gpu_util"` branch that
    forwards to `self.gpu_monitor.apply_event(evt)` (no-op if the
    widget doesn't exist, so headless tests aren't broken).
  * `_GPUMonitor.apply_event(evt)` parses the message and updates the
    three live labels (`util`, `mem`, `temp`). The widget still polls
    pynvml locally every second; the worker-emitted events provide a
    second source that's accurate to the GPU's actual workload (useful
    on shared GPUs or when the pynvml counter is coarse).
  * Parser is deliberately tolerant: malformed messages log nothing and
    leave the previous label value in place.

Tests (apps/anime_upscaler_gui/tests/test_telemetry_and_defaults.py, 8 new):
  * tensors.py enables allow_tf32 on matmul + cudnn when CUDA is up.
  * Defaults.prefetch is now "async".
  * _read_gpu_util returns a dict with util / mem_used_mb / mem_total_mb /
    temp_c (or None on truly GPU-less test boxes).
  * _read_gpu_util is process-cached (identity-stable across calls).
  * _maybe_emit_gpu_util suppresses emissions within 1 Hz of the previous
    one even when poked faster.
  * gpu_util message format is parseable by apply_event.
  * _GPUMonitor.apply_event updates util / mem / temp labels from a
    JobEvent.
  * _GPUMonitor.apply_event tolerates empty / garbage messages without
    raising.

Validation
----------
  * pytest: 325 passed, 13 warnings -- was 317 / 13 at Q3 baseline;
    +8 Q4 tests, zero new failures, same warning count.
  * Smoke: tmp/mid2s_job.py produces YiRenZhiXia_E02_mid2s_x4.mp4
    (575701 bytes) -- output unchanged. Wall time 95.6s (vs 102.7s
    at Q3 baseline, ~7% faster) -- the async default is doing real
    work even on a 36-frame clip.
